WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.879
Film Courage: Do you think it’s a mistake
for a filmmaker to think about distribution

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:05.440
after they’ve made the movie?

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.470
Mark Heidelberger, producer: Yes.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:13.270
I absolutely, 100 percent think it is a mistake
to only start thinking about marketing and

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.420
distribution after the film is done.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.109
And again, I’m going to say more things
that are going to piss people off…is I really

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:20.000
think the film schools have it all wrong.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.190
And I mentioned that guy that I spoke with
recently who I just feel like I wouldn’t

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.900
get along with and he’s one of these guys
who is “I’m just doing it for the passion.”

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:35.270
And I explained to him there is a reason we
call it show business and no show-show (and

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:38.390
I stole that from Peter Guber by the way)
who was one of my professors at UCLA.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.010
He used to say that “It’s not show-show.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.520
It’s show business.”

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.050
If you just want to go out and make a piece
of art for yourself and you have the money

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.379
to blow, you know go ahead.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:48.880
But that doesn’t mean you are going to be
part of the entertainment industry.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.930
That doesn’t mean you are going to be part
of the film…business.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.260
It is a business, it is a commodity as well
as art.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.420
That’s where people get hung up.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:00.550
It’s art right, but it’s art but it’s
also a commodity and people sacrifice one

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.550
for the other.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.290
And I read an article a couple months ago
and it just…oh my God…my head…it was

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:07.680
like a cartoon my head was just like [makes
a loud noise as if steam is going out].

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.890
I was so annoyed with this article.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.930
It just started with “Okay, now you’ve
finished your film.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.230
Now it’s time to start thinking about distribution.”

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.230
I’m like “No!

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.230
No!

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.230
It’s not!

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.240
It’s time to start thinking about distribution
ages ago.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.340
From the script stage you should have been
thinking about distribution.”

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.689
And I’ve told filmmakers that I’ve worked
with, the smart way to do it is (and they

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:32.829
always have a script that they really want
to do) and I say have you considered your

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:33.829
audience?

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:34.829
Have you considered whether there’s an audience
for it?

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:36.150
Have you considered how you are going to get
that audience?

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.590
Have you considered how big that audience
is and whether it justifies the budget you

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.910
want to spend and etc., etc.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:46.700
Have you looked t the market and seen what
other films are just like it in the market,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.330
how over-saturated it is.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.700
I can’t tell you how many times I’ve heard
people say “I’m going to do horror because

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.700
it’s so cheap.”

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.070
So you can make a bunch of money off of it
but you are only considering one thing, you

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.240
are only considering budget.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:01.110
You are not considering drawbacks of horror
like when you go to AFM [American Film Market]

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.969
there are 18 other films with posters that
look exactly like yours, all competing for

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:10.450
that same shelf space or that distributor
who only has a budget to acquire so many films.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.460
You are not considering the fact that a lot
of name talent doesn’t like to do horror,

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.900
which I was up against on my latest film THE
BASEMENT.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.450
Even casting directors know that, a lot of
casting directors don’t want to touch horror

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.560
because they know a lot of name actors just
avoid horror movies.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.980
Which is why so many times you see horror
movies with nobody in it you really recognize.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.970
Unless it’s like really smart, different
kind of horror, maybe you see some people

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:34.970
take a chance on it.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.310
But they are not necessarily looking at it
like a business.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:43.120
I tell people there is no other industry in
which you would spend millions of millions

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:50.790
of millions on R&amp;D and developing widgets
and mass-producing widgets “I’m going

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.910
to set up a big manufacturing space (a big
warehouse), I’m going to make widgets and

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.500
I’ve got this whole staff of people boxing
up widgets.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.450
And then when I’m done I’m going to have
this whole warehouse of widgets.”

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.909
And then when I’m done I’m like “Okay,
now let me figure out how I can sell these.”

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:03.970
No, you identify a need in the market.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:10.569
Like hey this…people are renting or buying
these kind of movies and there is definitely

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:14.560
an opportunity here.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.050
And I have a great story I think I can tell
within that.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.900
And consulting with distributors and sales
agents that know the market place.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:28.650
And if you’re afraid to get the distributors
and sales agents involved early and find out

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:34.909
what the market looks like and what buyers
are looking for and what budget ranges those

00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:37.879
pictures are in and which ones are making
money, if you don’t to do that and then

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:43.060
you make a picture and make no money you know
you really don’t have anyone to blame but

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.060
yourself.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:48.940
It’s absolutely the end use and the marketing,
distribution and publicity, festivals all

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:54.220
that, you should be looking at that from the
beginning, from inception of the story.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.860
And so a lot of times it falls on deaf ears.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.909
I always advise filmmakers “You should get
sales agents and distributors involved from

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:01.909
the get-go.”

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:06.880
I’m not saying it’s going to make it fool-proof
that you are going to make a return-on-your-investment

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.390
because of that, but it’s going to (I think)
get you a lot closer.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:13.470
Are they always right?

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.470
No.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:15.920
I mean they can be wrong as well.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.590
But they know a lot more than we do.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.579
Just like you trust a DP to shoot the picture
because he knows more about lighting and camera

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:22.900
than you do.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.919
Or an editor because he knows a lot more about
cutting than you do.

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:29.230
Distributors and sales agents know more about
selling in the market than you do.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.710
And so if you want some chance at getting
some sort of better return, involve people

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:36.220
that know more about what they do than you
do.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.990
And I say this all the time when I hire people.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.330
I say “Look, I hired you because you know
more about what you do than I know about what

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:42.330
you do.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.330
So I’m going to tell you the result I want.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:46.139
I’m going to tell you when I want it by
and I’m going to tell you what resources

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:47.639
I’m going to give you to give me that result.

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:49.550
And I you say ‘I can do it.’

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.040
I’m going to say “Great, go do it then.”

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.380
And I’m here to support you, you tell me
hey I need you to do this or that as long

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.730
as it is within those parameters, done.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:02.720
If there’s a problem, we’ll fix it and
we’ll make sure it doesn’t happen again.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.000
So trust but verify but I don’t stand over
their shoulders making sure…I hired you

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.780
because this is what you do and you’re better
at it than I am.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:11.870
So go do it.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.970
And the crew loves that.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.680
The sales agents and distributors, treat them
like part of your team because by the time

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.169
people have made their movie and come to the
distributor, the distributors are like “Well

00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:22.750
there is not much we can do with it now.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.550
We can try to craft a marketing campaign.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.979
But if you had this person in it and if you
had only spent this much and if you had done

00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:33.030
these things with the story, we’d be in
a lot better of a position.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.721
Now all we have, we didn’t have any say
in the story or script, we didn’t have any

00:05:37.721 --> 00:05:41.970
say in the casting, we didn’t have any say
in the budget, now all we have is the marketing

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:42.970
campaign.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.350
But we’re stuck with the movie we have,
there’s not much we can do with it now.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:52.919
So I am absolutely a fan of people getting
distributors and sales agents or talking to

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:53.919
them, producer’s reps.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:56.550
Whoever you can that knows the marketplace,
get them involved as early as possible.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.090
 

